[Event "A Gambit Repertoire for White: Introduction"]
[Date "2021.06.28"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "A Gambit Repertoire for White"]
[ChapterName "Introduction"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/D8QPC8Zr/OfE3zg87"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/OnlyForStudies"]
[UTCDate "2021.06.28"]
[UTCTime "01:11:53"]

{ Chess nowadays is getting real boring. Openings are being studied to the bone; up to move 30 and into the endgame like the Berlin and the Sicilian Najdorf. This can lead to lack of creativity and style, which is why nowadays anyone from 8-99 can become a grandmaster easily, unlike in the 60's or 70's. Chess needs to be spiced up now, and I have perfect solution: Gambits! Gambits are truly the way to go in Chess to win in style and have fun at the same time. But I am not going to recommend to you some boring King's gambit or Smith Morra, I am going to recommend unexplored gambits and openings that play in the gambit style. So, will all that being said, lets begin!

(Note: This study is based on the white gambit repertoire by Eric Schiller, but also has my own analyse and lines in it as well.) }
 *


