1. e4 { The starting move for this repertoire because almost all gambits come from e4. } 1... c5 (1... c5 2. d4 cxd4 3. f4 { This is the Halasz Gambit, and it's plan is to attack Black's kingside fast with h3, Qe1-g3/h4, and g4 followed up with f5; and also expand on the queenside with a3 and b4. In this gambit we really don't care about the d4 pawn, so we will develop around it. }) (1... e5 2. d4 exd4 3. f4 { Same as with the Sicilian, we're also playing the halasz gambit here too. The plan is also the same with the kingsie attack. } (3. c3 { White can also play the Danish gambit, a 19th century romantic two pawn gambit that aims for rapid development and quick, tactical attacks towards the king })) (1... e6 2. d4 d5 3. Be3 { In this line we're gambiting a pawn in exchange for rapid development, which is also seen in the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit }) (1... c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nf3 { This is a gambit that is a lot like the budapest and tennison gambit combined. White may not gain back the pawn, but he/she will have a lot of attacking prospects. }) (1... d5 2. Nf3 { A simple and easy gambit to play for a white; this is a pretty solid line. }) (1... Nf6 2. Bc4 { A nice gambit which stops Black from castling
1...Nc6/g6/d6/b6 don't have gambits associated with them that are good }) 2. d4 cxd4 3. f4 { This is the Halasz Gambit, and it's plan is to attack Black's kingside fast with h3, Qe1-g3/h4, and g4 followed up with f5; and also expand on the queenside with a3 and b4. In this gambit we really don't care about the d4 pawn, so we will develop around it. } *


[Event "A Gambit Repertoire for White: The Sicilian Halasz Gambit"]
[Date "2021.06.28"]
[Result "*"]
