[StudyName "A Gambit Repertoire for White"]
[ChapterName "The Sicilian Halasz Gambit"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/D8QPC8Zr/nxtTSa79"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/OnlyForStudies"]
[UTCDate "2021.06.28"]
[UTCTime "01:32:43"]

1. e4 c5 { A move you're most likely going to see 50% of the time in all of your chess games as White. } 2. d4 cxd4 3. f4 { With this move we signal a Kingside attack( h3, Qe1-g3/h4, and g4 followed up with f5; and also expand on the queenside with a3 and b4) and control many important central squares, but our plan is to control f5. In the Halasz gambit, the d4 pawn is going to be kept there because we don't want to transpose to other Sicilians. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge4f5,Ge4d5,Gf4e5,Gf4g5] } 3... d6 (3... d6 4. Nf3 Bg4 { stopping Qxd4 } (4... Nf6 5. Bd3 { protecting the pawn and not cring about the d4 pawn } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] } 5... Nc6 6. O-O e5 (6... Bg4 { transposition }) 7. h3 { preparing the advancement of g4 and stopping Black from coming to g4 } 7... Be7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Nc4 { followed by g4 and the queen moving to either g3 or h4 }) 5. Bd3! { A key idea that needs to be remembered: White doesn't need to care about the d4 pawn and should instead develop around it. This Bd3 move is also seen in the Elephant Gambit; Maroczy variation. } (5. Qxd4 Bxf3 6. gxf3 { ugly doubled, hanging pawns } 6... Nc6 { and this is good for Black }) 5... Nc6 6. O-O { we don't need to worry bout ...Nb4 ideas because they're useless. } 6... Nf6 7. h3 { Forcing the trade and also expanding on the kingside } 7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 (8... e5 9. Bc4 { White now has compensation because Black position is weird. }) 9. Nd2 Be7 10. a3 { And we start the queenside expansion } 10... O-O { Simply expanding on the Kingside and following the kingside and queenside expansion plan. I would say that both sides, with human play, have equal chances, although the computer would disagree }) (3... Nc6 4. Nf3 Qb6 { protecting the pawn } (4... g6 { not the best move to make because of the e4 and f5 pawns that can attack g6. } 5. Bd3 { again, not caring about the pawn on d4 } 5... Bg7 6. O-O Nf6 7. Qe1 { The arrows show the plan against this set-up. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Ge1h4,Gf4f5,Gc1h6,Bh2h3,Bg2g4] } 7... d5 8. e5 Ne4 9. Nbd2 Nxd2 10. Bxd2 Qb6 11. Qg3 { I would just accept this perpetual line despite the irony towards this study. } 11... Qxb2 12. Rfb1 Qa3 13. Rb3 Qc5 14. Rb5 Qa3 15. Rb3 $10) (4... d5 { not that good of a move by Black since, in this line, we have to gin the pawn back an Black doesn't hve much compensation for the pawn give. } 5. exd5 (5. e5 { this move is recommended by Halasz Gambiteers, but I don't like White's position after this. }) 5... Qxd5 6. Nc3 { Black can't take c3 due to the line against the queen } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd1d5,Gc3d5,Rd4c3] } 6... Qe6+ (6... Qd8 7. Nb5 { gainign the pawn back } 7... a6 (7... Qa5+ 8. Qd2) 8. Nbxd4) 7. Ne2 Nf6 8. Nxd4 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 { and white is better }) 5. Bd3 { You should now understand why this is played } 5... d6 (5... Nf6 6. O-O d5 7. e5 Ne4 8. Nbd2 Nc5 9. Nb3 Nxd3 10. cxd3 Bf5 11. Nh4 Bd7 12. Qe2 e6 13. f5 Nb4 14. fxe6 Bxe6 15. Nf5 { Halasz-Nilsson, Postal 1976 }) 6. O-O g6 7. Nbd2 Bg7 8. a3 { This move isn't to stop Black's ...Nb4 ideas, but to control and expand on the queenside } 8... Nf6 9. h3 { Kingside expansion ideas } 9... e5 10. b4 a6 11. g4 { now that we control the queenside, let's control the kingside } 11... O-O 12. Qe1 Qc7 13. f5 { because of g6, we must attack with f5 } 13... d5 14. Qg3 dxe4 15. Nxe4 Nd5 16. fxg6 hxg6 17. g5 $13) 4. Nf3 Bg4 { stopping Qxd4 } (4... Nf6 5. Bd3 { protecting the pawn and not cring about the d4 pawn } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] } 5... Nc6 6. O-O e5 (6... Bg4 { transposition }) 7. h3 { preparing the advancement of g4 and stopping Black from coming to g4 } 7... Be7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Nc4 { followed by g4 and the queen moving to either g3 or h4 }) 5. Bd3! { A key idea that needs to be remembered: White doesn't need to care about the d4 pawn and should instead develop around it. This Bd3 move is also seen in the Elephant Gambit; Maroczy variation. } (5. Qxd4 Bxf3 6. gxf3 { ugly doubled, hanging pawns } 6... Nc6 { and this is good for Black }) 5... Nc6 6. O-O { we don't need to worry bout ...Nb4 ideas because they're useless. } 6... Nf6 7. h3 { Forcing the trade and also expanding on the kingside } 7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 (8... e5 9. Bc4 { White now has compensation because Black position is weird. }) 9. Nd2 Be7 10. a3 { And we start the queenside expansion } 10... O-O { Simply expanding on the Kingside and following the kingside and queenside expansion plan. I would say that both sides, with human play, have equal chances, although the computer would disagree } *


