[StudyName "A Gambit Repertoire for White"]
[ChapterName "The Central Halasz Gambit"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/D8QPC8Zr/BgVAEZoQ"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/OnlyForStudies"]
[UTCDate "2021.06.28"]
[UTCTime "12:46:50"]

1. e4 e5 2. d4 { Black does sometimes decline the center game with d6 and Nc6, but both are easy to play against. } 2... exd4 3. f4 { As also seen in the sicilian, White's kingside attack is very natural here, and there are now two line's we're going to look at:
A( 1 e4 e5 2 d4 exd4 3 f4 Bc5 4 Nf3 Nc6 5 a3 a5 6 Bd3 Nf6 7 0-0)
B( 3...Bb4+)
This halasz version is a little harder to play with so be careful with this and be prepared.
Note(This chapter is based on https://www.chesscafe.com/text/halasz.txt and also has my own lines and analysis in it as well) } 3... Bc5 (3... Nc6 4. Nf3 Bc5 { transposes to the mainline }) (3... Bb4+ 4. Nd2 Nf6 (4... Nc6 5. a3 Bc5 6. b4 Bb6 7. Ngf3 Nf6 8. Bd3) 5. Bd3 d5 6. e5 Ng4 7. Nf3 { and white gets normal play in the Halasz }) 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. a3 { With a3, also in the sicilian Halasz, White is trying to expand on the queenside with b4 and stop any pieces from going to b4 as well. } 5... a5 { Stopping b4 } 6. Bd3 { You should know why this move is played because of the Sicilian Halasz, White doesn't care about the d4 pawn and develops around it. This move is also seen in the Elephant Gambit. } 6... Nf6 7. O-O { Black now has two main moves: 7...d5 and 7...d6 or 7...Ne following up with 8...d5/d6. } 7... d5 (7... d5 8. e5 { White has to play this to start expanding on the kingside. Black cn now either play Ne4 or Ng4 } (8. exd5 Qxd5 { and Black is too good }) 8... Ng4 (8... Ne4 9. Nbd2 Nxd2 10. Bxd2 { White is threatening kingside assualts if Black castles } { [%cal Gd3h7,Gf3g5] } 10... O-O { he did it anyways } (10... Ne7 { tro prevent f5 } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge7f5,Gc8f5,Bd3f5,Bf4f5] }) 11. f5 { and now the attack begins } 11... Ne7 12. Ng5 { Leads to a messy position } 12... f6 13. Qh5 fxg5 14. f6 Ng6 { blocking the checkmate } 15. f7+ Rxf7 16. Rxf7 Kxf7 17. Qxh7 Nxe5 18. Rf1+ Ke6 19. Qxg7 Nxd3 20. cxd3 $10) 9. Qe2 Ne3 { [%csl Gf1][%cal Ge3f1] } 10. Bxe3 dxe3 11. Nc3 d4 12. Ne4 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge4c5] } 12... Ba7 13. Nfg5 h5 14. h3 Bf5 15. Nxf7! Kxf7 16. Ng5+ { [%csl Gf5,Gf6,Gd6,Ge6][%cal Gd3f5,Ge5d6,Ge5f6,Gg5e6] } 16... Ke8 (16... Kg6 17. Bxf5+ Kxf5 18. Qd3#) 17. Bxf5 { and White went on to win in Salminen - Lapine, 1994 corres. }) (7... d6 8. Qe1 { Getting ready for the Kingside expansion with h3 and g4 and Qg3/h4 seen in the Sicilian Halasz as well. } (8. e5 Nd5 { Not a good plan for white } { [%csl Ge3,Gg4][%cal Gd5e3,Gc8g4] }) 8... O-O { black has to be ready to meet e5 because of the kingsie attack of White } { [%cal Ge4e5] } 9. h3 { [%csl Gg3,Gh4,Gg4][%cal Gg2g4,Ge1g3,Gg3h4] } 9... Bd7 10. e5 Re8 11. Qg3 Nh5 12. Qh2 g6 13. g4 Ng7 14. Nbd2 f5 15. exf6 Qxf6 16. Ne4 Qd8 17. Qg3 Re7 18. Qh4 { A typical Halasz position in Both Halasz versions: Black has an extra pawn, but he is pinned down by a White attack. } 18... Ne8 19. f5! { White's advantage is obvious and he went on to win in 26 moves in Blank -
Wothe, 1991 corres. }) (7... O-O?? 8. e5 { Greek gift sac and Black is dead lost. } { [%csl Gh7,Gg5,Gh5][%cal Gd3h7,Gf3g5,Gf4g5,Gd1h5] }) 8. e5 { White has to play this to start expanding on the kingside. Black cn now either play Ne4 or Ng4 } (8. exd5 Qxd5 { and Black is too good }) 8... Ng4 (8... Ne4 9. Nbd2 Nxd2 10. Bxd2 { White is threatening kingside assualts if Black castles } { [%cal Gd3h7,Gf3g5] } 10... O-O { he did it anyways } (10... Ne7 { tro prevent f5 } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge7f5,Gc8f5,Bd3f5,Bf4f5] }) 11. f5 { and now the attack begins } 11... Ne7 12. Ng5 { Leads to a messy position } 12... f6 13. Qh5 fxg5 14. f6 Ng6 { blocking the checkmate } 15. f7+ Rxf7 16. Rxf7 Kxf7 17. Qxh7 Nxe5 18. Rf1+ Ke6 19. Qxg7 Nxd3 20. cxd3 $10) 9. Qe2 Ne3 { [%csl Gf1][%cal Ge3f1] } 10. Bxe3 dxe3 11. Nc3 d4 12. Ne4 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ge4c5] } 12... Ba7 13. Nfg5 h5 14. h3 Bf5 15. Nxf7! Kxf7 16. Ng5+ { [%csl Gf5,Gf6,Gd6,Ge6][%cal Gd3f5,Ge5d6,Ge5f6,Gg5e6] } 16... Ke8 (16... Kg6 17. Bxf5+ Kxf5 18. Qd3#) 17. Bxf5 { and White went on to win in Salminen - Lapine, 1994 corres. } *


