1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Be3 { With this gambit, white wants rapid development and nice piece placement in exchange for a pawn. } 3... dxe4 (3... Nf6 { Black can always decline } 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 { and White id doing fine here }) 4. Nd2 { The correct Knight move, trying to attack the pawn. } (4. Nc3 Bb4 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Gb4e1] }) 4... Nf6 (4... f5 { following Keres vs Verbac } 5. f3 exf3 6. Ngxf3 Nf6 7. Bd3 c5 8. O-O cxd4 9. Nxd4 { already White has developed about everything } 9... f4 10. Rxf4! { not even caring about the fork because White has a major attack coming } 10... e5 11. Bb5+ Kf7 { The move played in the game } (11... Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Bxc6+ Bd7 14. Bxa8 Qxa8 15. Rf1) (11... Bd7 12. Rxf6 Qxf6 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Qe2 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Ge3g5,Ge2e8] } 14... Bxb5 15. Nxb5 { [%cal Gb5c7] }) 12. Qh5+ { [%csl Gf7,Gh5][%cal Gf4f7,Rf6h5] } 12... g6 (12... Kg8 13. Bc4+ Nd5 14. Qf7#) 13. Bc4+ Kg7 14. Qh6+ Kxh6 15. Rh4+ Kg7 16. Bh6#) 5. f3 { Black can decline the second gambit } 5... exf3 (5... Nd5 6. Qe2 exf3 7. Ngxf3 { This is also fine for white as well considdering how much he/she has deved. }) 6. Ngxf3 Be7 (6... c5 7. dxc5 Qc7 8. Nb3 (8. b4)) 7. Bd3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 { And this is where the theory ends. This is a very little know gambit, and it is up to you to decide how the game should progress. For more info. on this gambit, please go here:
http://www.nic.funet.fi/pub/doc/games/chess/ddg/Gambits/alapin.html } *


