1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nf3 { A very new gambit with ideas from the tennison gambit. Black really can't decline this gambit unless he/she likes equality } 3... dxe4 4. Ng5 { White wants to gain back the pawn in this fashion in both this and the the Tennison Gambit. } 4... Nf6 (4... Bf5 { a sensible alternative } 5. c3 (5. f3 exf3 6. Nxf3 { a tempoless diemer gambit }) 5... e6 (5... h6 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Qb3+ e6 8. Qxb7+ Nd7 9. Qxc6 { And White has full compensation for the piece }) 6. Qb3 { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gb3b7] } 6... Qd7 7. Nd2 Nf6 8. Nc4 { This gives white full compensation } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gc4e5] }) 5. Bc4 { A key move to remember in this gambit because it immediatly attacks f7 and shuts down Black's light-squared bishop, unlike Nc3. } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7,Gg5f7] } 5... e6 6. Nc3 { 2 v 1 on the pawn } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc3e4,Gg5e4,Bf6e4] } 6... Bb4 { 2 v 2 now because Black pins our knight and the pawn cannot be attacked anymore } (6... Bb4 { 2 v 2 now because Black pins our knight and the pawn cannot be attacked anymore } 7. O-O (7. Qe2?? Qxd4) 7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 h6 9. Nh3 { Because the e-pawn is safe, White now has to fight to justify the gambit } 9... Qa5 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Ga5c3] } (9... Nbd7 10. Bb3 e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. Bf4 Nf3+ 14. gxf3 Bxh3 15. Rfd1+ Kc8 16. fxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxf7 Rf8 18. Rd4 { and white is fine here }) (9... O-O 10. f3 exf3 (10... Qa5 11. Qe1 { transposition }) 11. Qxf3) 10. Qe1 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Ge1c3,Ga5c3] } (10. Bd2 e5 11. f3 Bxh3 12. gxh3 e3 13. Re1) 10... O-O 11. f3 c5 (11... exf3 12. Rxf3) 12. fxe4 (12. dxc5 { kills our pawn sturcture }) 12... Nxe4 13. Qxe4 Qxc3 14. Bxh6! gxh6 15. dxc5 $16 { protects the bishop and white is alot better here } { [%cal Gf1f8,Ge4h7,Gc4g8] } 15... Nc6 16. Rae1 Rd8 17. Rxf7 Kxf7 18. Qh7+ Ke8 19. Qg8+ Kd7 20. Qf7+ Ne7 21. Rd1+ Kc7 22. Qxe7+ Rd7 23. Rxd7+ Bxd7 24. Qd6+ Kd8 25. Qf8+ $10) (6... Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. Ngxe4 Nxe4 9. Nxe4 $14) (6... b5 7. Bb3 a5 8. a4! Bb4 9. O-O Bxc3 10. bxc3 h6 11. Nh3 $14 { The bishop pair and developmet make white better here, but black is up a pawn so white is slightly better } 11... O-O 12. f3 Ba6 13. axb5 cxb5 14. fxe4 Nxe4 15. Qg4 Nf6 $10 16. Qh4 { With the idea of Bxh6 sacrifice } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gc1h6] } 16... Nbd7 17. Bxh6 gxh6 18. Rf3 Nh7 19. Qxh6 Kh8 20. Raf1 Qe7 21. Qh5 b4 22. Rxf7 Rxf7 23. Rxf7 Qxf7 24. Qxf7 Rf8 25. Qxf8+ Nhxf8 26. cxb4 axb4 27. Nf4 Nf6 28. Nxe6 Nxe6 29. Bxe6 $10) (6... Nbd7 7. O-O h6 (7... Bd6 8. Re1 Qc7 9. h3 O-O 10. Ngxe4) 8. Ngxe4 Nxe4 9. Nxe4 Nf6 10. Qd3! { just developing the queen } 10... Be7 11. Be3 Qc7 12. Rad1 $16 { And white stands better here }) 7. O-O (7. Qe2?? Qxd4) 7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 h6 9. Nh3 { Because the e-pawn is safe, White now has to fight to justify the gambit } 9... Qa5 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Ga5c3] } (9... Nbd7 10. Bb3 e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. Bf4 Nf3+ 14. gxf3 Bxh3 15. Rfd1+ Kc8 16. fxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxf7 Rf8 18. Rd4 { and white is fine here }) (9... O-O 10. f3 exf3 (10... Qa5 11. Qe1 { transposition }) 11. Qxf3) 10. Qe1 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Ge1c3,Ga5c3] } (10. Bd2 e5 11. f3 Bxh3 12. gxh3 e3 13. Re1) 10... O-O 11. f3 c5 (11... exf3 12. Rxf3) 12. fxe4 (12. dxc5 { kills our pawn sturcture }) 12... Nxe4 13. Qxe4 Qxc3 14. Bxh6! gxh6 15. dxc5 $16 { protects the bishop and white is alot better here } { [%cal Gf1f8,Ge4h7,Gc4g8] } 15... Nc6 16. Rae1 Rd8 17. Rxf7 Kxf7 18. Qh7+ Ke8 19. Qg8+ Kd7 20. Qf7+ Ne7 21. Rd1+ Kc7 22. Qxe7+ Rd7 23. Rxd7+ Bxd7 24. Qd6+ Kd8 25. Qf8+ $10 *


