1. e4 d5 2. Nf3 { Also known as the intercontinental Ballistic Missle Gambit, the Tennison gambit is meant for getting a nice, colid position out pof the opening. this opening can also be a system for white if he/she where to play 1. Nf3 on the first move. If Black defends, we can transpose to other openings } 2... dxe4 (2... c6 3. d4 { The Ulysses Gambit }) 3. Ng5 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gg5e4] } 3... Nf6 (3... Bf5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bc4 e6 6. Qe2 Qd4 7. Nb5 Qb6 8. Nc3 Qd4 { This is the line i recommend }) (3... f5 4. Bc4 { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7,Gg5f7] } 4... Nh6 (4... e6 5. Nxe6 Bxe6 6. Bxe6 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Ge6f5] } 6... Qf6 7. Bb3) 5. Nxh7 Rxh7 6. Qh5+ Kd7 7. Qg6 { [%csl Ge6,Gh7][%cal Gg6e6,Gg6h7,Gc4e6] } 7... Rh8 (7... c6 8. Qxh7) 8. Be6+ Kc6 9. Bxc8+ Qd6 10. Qe8+ Kb6 11. Na3 { and black is dead } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ga3c4] }) (3... Qd5 4. d3 exd3 5. Bxd3 { here we don't even care about g2 } 5... Nf6 6. O-O h6 7. Nc3 Qa5 8. Nge4 Nxe4 9. Nxe4 { and white is developing fine here }) (3... e5 { The correct line against } 4. Nxe4 f5 (4... Nf6 5. Nxf6+ Qxf6 6. Nc3 { and play continues }) 5. Ng3 Nf6 6. Bc4 { and black will now find it hard to castle } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Gc4g8] } 6... Nc6 7. O-O) 4. Bc4 { The correct way to play this gambit, simply attacking f7 and dev. pieces } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7,Gg5f7] } (4. d3 { This is what is recommended by IM Levy Rozman, but I don't like it because Black can easily refute this line even if Black were 800 } 4... exd3 5. Bxd3 e6 { and Black is fine } (5... h6 { Obviously we know that this is the trap } 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Bg6+ { you'll never get this trap online })) 4... e6 5. Nc3 { And the pawn is now attacked 2 times. If black were to develop the bishops or knights, then take the pawn with the g-knight } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gg5e4,Gc3e4] } 5... Qd4 { The last chance to hold onto the pawn } 6. Qe2 Nc6 7. Ngxe4 Nxe4 8. Qxe4 Qxe4+ 9. Nxe4 Nb4 10. Bb3 a5 11. a3 Nd5 12. O-O { and the game continues from here } *


