1. e4 Nf6 2. Bc4! { White can just avoid the alekhine's defense with this move } 2... Nxe4 { Black can always decline the gambit with e5, but I would just transpose into the Frankenstein-Deacula Gambit } (2... e5 3. Nc3 Nxe4 { Black tries to fork } 4. Qh5! { An excellent intermiediate move } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gh5f7,Gc4f7] } 4... Nd6 (4... g6 5. Qxe5+ Qe7 6. Qxe7+ (6. Qxh8 { too risky } { [%csl Ge1][%cal Ge7e1] }) 6... Bxe7 7. Nxe4) (4... Ng5 5. d4 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gc1g5] } 5... Ne6 { blocks f7 } { [%csl Re6][%cal Gc4f7] } 6. dxe5 { And white is better }) 5. Qxe5+ Qe7 6. Qxe7+ Bxe7 7. Bb3 { and white is bettrer here due to the on d6 blocking the bishop }) 3. Bxf7+ { The only way to gain the pawn back } 3... Kxf7 4. Qh5+ g6 (4... Kg8 5. Qd5+ { is the same }) (4... Ke6 { This is too risky for Black }) 5. Qd5+ e6 6. Qxe4 Bg7 7. Nf3 Rf8 8. d4 Kg8 9. Bg5 Qe8 $14 { And this is the end of the chapter. This is a very simple gambit that can help you gain an advantege out of the poening } (9... Bf6 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. Nc3) *


